f making people who  make  of getting people who make decisions  have an incentive to make the right  decision  and on the market we do that pretty well  because if you want to  produce something you've got to pay for  your input so you've got to compensate  for the people who are providing those  inputs for the cost to them of the  inputs  you get paid for your output so at least  roughly speaking  the net gain to you is the net gain to  everybody the value of what you've  reduced minus the cost  people are producing it that's not true  in the political system in the political  system  most people who make decisions neither  bear the cost  nor receive the benefit of the decision  and the result is that political  decisions tend to be made  for reasons that have very little to do  with whether they actually make us  better off and i could give  lots of examples but that's sort of the  general argument  the particular the detailed point for an  anarcho-capitalism  is to apply that argument to law as well  as everything else  the first part of the argument i think a  lot of conservatives would on the whole  agree with uh that their socialism works  badly capitalism works well it's  not everybody agrees with it obviously  but it's a fairly widespread belief  but most people believe that fundamental  things like law and law enforcement have  to be done by government  and the inaugural capitalist view is  that for the same reason governments do  a bad job of producing food  with the result that when governments  are in charge of producing and  distributing food there is likely to be  a famine has happened a couple of times  in my lifetime  well once in my lifetime once just  before my lifetime actually  uh that similarly if governments are in  charge of making law  they're likely to do a bad job of making  law uh  and again one could give examples and  that  therefore if you could find a way of  getting laws made on the market the same  way we  grow potatoes you would be more likely  to get high quality laws and i spent a  good deal of time during my talk  sketching the institutions which would  at least you know which might we don't  really know what will happen but which  might  function to produce the fundamental  government functions privately  how achievable is this uh  in my lifetime almost certainly not in  your lifetime maybe  uh but i think where it's most likely to  happen is not  the world as a whole but those parts of  the world that is hard for governments  to control  so in particular i think that cyberspace  is developing at least some features of  inaugural capitalism  that for example you have contract  enforcement  done mainly by reputation rather than  mainly by legal force  if you look at how ebay organizes things  that basically when you buy something  from ebay  you report to ebay on how it turned out  when someone else wants to buy for the  same seller he sees whether or not most  buyers were satisfied  they've got various mechanisms of that  sort which are avoiding the whole  government legal system  and using the alternative of reputation  which is the sort of thing i would hope  to see  uh and more generally online  national borders are invisible  governments have limited ability to keep  track of what's happening  so i think there's a real possibility  even in my lifetime  that you could have something rather  like a narco capitalism  for the limited world of online commerce  but for more than that i think unless  they solve the aging problem which i  have more in favor of they're doing  every year for obvious reasons  but unless they solve that i don't think  that it's likely to happen in my  lifetime  but i think it's worth putting ideas out  and when you put ideas out eventually  someone may implement them  ronald cost who was a prominent  economist  proposed way back maybe in the 1950s  that instead of having the government  allocate the airwaves  say this radio station is allowed to  broadcast on this frequencies well that  one you should just auction them off  treat them as property and let whatever  radio station  uh could make the most money out of  broadcasting in a particular place by it  and at the time coast made the argument  was obviously an absurd idea nobody  would ever do  and currently it's government policy now  it's not quite as an extreme aversion as  coasters because the current government  policy  they decide what category a particular  frequency range has to be used for  but then they auction off that frequency  range to the highest bidder  so that would be one example i think  where what seemed like a  absurdly optimistic or  unrealistic view happened and in the  other direction in the direction of  things i don't like  uh it's arguable that the most  successful political party in america  was the socialist party  it never won any important elections i  think it elected a mayor of milwaukee  once and that's about the limit  but if you compare their platform  beginning of the 20th century  with the republican the democratic  platforms at the end of the 20th century  you discover that they largely won  and they did it by making certain ideas  popular i think many of them were bad  ideas in my view but that doesn't mean  they didn't work  politically and therefore other people  ended up  implementing those ideas so i guess what  i'm doing is not i'm not  a revolutionary or a reformer i'm not  trying to create institutions  i'm trying to create ideas in the hopes  that other people will eventually make  use of it  where has the closest governmental  system  to that's interesting historically  speaking  there are a fair number of relatively  prim  not primitive made who's strong but of  early societies  in which law enforcement was private and  decentralized  and the one i know best and wrote about  earliest  is saga period iceland this is iceland  about a thousand years ago  and that was a society where there was a  legislature and a court system uh  but there was no government enforcement  there was no executive arm of government  so after you got your verdict it was  basically up to you to enforce it  and that's the system that lasted for  more than 300 years that eventually  broke down as most  systems have it produced a lot of very  fine literature  we get our word saga from the icelandic  sagas which were  historical novels and histories written  in the 13th and 14th century  and if you think about how many of the  sagas are still in print in english  translation  it really puts iceland on a per capita  basis on the same grounds as berkeley  and athens or elizabethan england  it's a tiny population and we're still  reading the things they wrote  700 years later quite a lot of them  so that would be one but there are other  examples that northern somalia  somaliland back before the creation of  the nation of somalia  which was created artificially by italy  and england that was a mistake i think  had essentially a less fancy version of  the kind of society i'm describing in  which there  was no government nominally the english  were in charge but mostly they weren't  and before that they weren't at all and  in which there were private mechanisms  for settling disputes and enforcing the  rules  and there are a number of other such  things my most recent non-fiction book  is a book called  legal systems very different from ours  in which i  look at a whole bunch of different legal  systems  and one of the f it came out of a  seminar i taught every other year at  santa clara university law school for  many years  and one of the fun things was that i  concluded that in my first book where i  sketched out how inaudible capitalism  might work  i was really reinventing the wheel that  is to say i was inventing  in the context of a modern high division  labor of labor kind of society  something a primitive version of which  had existed a thousand years ago  uh probably more than that in various  places  so that's i mean that would be one of  the places  but i don't think you really have an  aqua capitalism  anywhere in the world today but you have  elements of it  that an awful lot of commercial disputes  are settled by private arbitration by  the american arbitration association and  similar groups  rather than by the courts there are  gated communities where people are in  effect providing their own  police protection so i think the answer  is that you've got  bits of it as it were interwoven in a  modern society but you don't have  anything that's a  full scale an aquacopter listen more  very close to it  and why is that why do you think that so  few places  that's a good question capitalism in the  way that you advocate  that's a good question uh i don't know  uh that's the best argument that it  doesn't work  or it isn't workable is that we don't  see it on the other hand if you go back  a couple of hundred years  and you imagine somewhere somewhere  around 1800  who's dreamed up this really weird  society i mean it's a society in which  government is so big  that it takes that it collects and  spends 40 percent of all income  it's a society where everybody can vote  even women  it's a if you go down the list of the  descriptions of the standard modern  western society  none of those existed in 1800  all right so my hope is that as  circumstances change in various ways  what wasn't workable a hundred years ago  may be workable 100 years hence but  we'll have to see  so what concrete steps would you  recommend  uh in the usa and the uk  uh to move towards this  utopia um  sure the i guess the obvious  steps are to make it easier to  privatize things now done by government  so that for example  in the us i'm in favor of a voucher  system for schooling  uh that there's no particular reason  even if we even if government is going  to pay for schooling which may or may  not be a good idea there's no reason why  they should control it  and therefore uh what you ought to do is  to say that if  the government is currently spending  twelve thousand dollars a year on each  school kid  which is about depends on where you are  but somewhere around there  that if a parent will arrange to have  the kid educated somewhere else they  should get that money to  be used to pay for the education and if  you do that i think over a period of  might i suppose 30 or 40 years you will  largely replace the public school system  with a  much more diverse private school system  with different kinds of schools doing  things in different ways  that would be one step in the right  direction uh in general i think that the  court system  should be more willing than it is to  recognize freedom of contracts to take  the position that  contracting parties are allowed to make  their own rules for themselves which  courts were willing to do pretty much in  1900 are much less willing to do in 2000  that would be one example  there are some areas where the  government is both doing bad things  and doing bad things which result  in indirect bad things as it were and  i'm thinking now mainly of the war on  drugs  that i think to begin with preventing  people from taking drugs is a bad thing  that you own your own body and it's up  to you what you put in it  but beyond that at least in the us the  war on drugs results  in no knock raids and all sorts of  unpleasantness where  the government law enforcement is doing  violent things they wouldn't have to do  if it weren't that they were trying to  suppress a very large uh and a lucrative  market  uh and i would have thought we would  have learned that lesson with  prohibition  back in the 1920s but apparently we  didn't  so those would be some of the things  that would move it in the right  direction  but beyond that i think  it isn't really government beyond that  the two things that have to be done or  what i'm doing  at the moment which is to try to spread  ideas  favorable to a less governmental system  and creating 